    "lawCode": "Amd §3343-b, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3687\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public health law, in relation to\nthe safe disposal of unused controlled substances\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nEnsures retail pharmacies and other Drug Enforcement Administration\nauthorized collectors can collect unused controlled substances in New\nYork.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends Public Health Law § 3343-b to provide that\npharmacies and other Federal Drug Enforcement Administration\nauthorized collectors can collect, on a voluntary basis, in New York.\nThe Department of Health shall oversee this program for safe disposal\nof unused controlled substances in accordance with federal law.\n\nSection two provides that this act shall take effect immediately\nnotwithstanding the failure to promulgate any rule or regulation\nproviding for the implementation thereof.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAccording to the National Survey on Drug Use and Health, 70% of\ncontrolled substance prescription abusers get their drugs from a\nfamily member or friend. Yet there are limited methods for individuals\nto dispose of their unused medications. Currently in New York,\nmedication drop boxes are only available at law enforcement agencies,\nand not all counties have law enforcement that participate in this\nprogram. Often to meet the need of communities, local groups working\ntogether with law enforcement officers, who must be present at the\nevent, host shed-the-med events that yield an astounding about of\nunused medication that would otherwise be on the streets.\n\nIn the continuing fight against opioid abuse, New York enacted Chapter\n343 of 2013 as an effort to increase avenues of drug disposal.\nChapter 343 was enacted in anticipation of federal changes allowing\nfor DEA registrants such as manufacturers, distributors, retail\npharmacies and hospitals/clinics with on-site pharmacies to become\nauthorized collectors, in addition to law enforcement agencies. The\nDrug Enforcement Administration (DEA) released there finalized\nregulations on September 8, 2014. Chapter 343 required the\nCommissioner to establish regulations relating to pharmacies within\nthe state drug disposal program, and enabled any pharmacy designated\nby the Commissioner to accept controlled substances. Today, many\npharmacies stand ready and willing to become disposals sites, unused\nmedications remain on the streets, and the Commissioner has yet to\npropose any draft regulations under this 2013 statute.\n\nThis legislation will amend the drug disposal program to allow\npharmacies, and other DEA authorized collectors, to begin functioning\nas collection sites immediately provided they are authorized by the\nDEA and in compliance with federal laws and regulations. The\nDepartment of Health remains responsible for the program and ensuring\nthat collectors are in compliance with all federal and state laws.\n\n\nThe need to address excess drugs on that street is long overdue, and\nwhile it must be done cautiously, it must also be addressed\nexpeditiously. Compliance with federal regulations should be\nsufficient in this circumstance and citizens should not be forced to\nsit idly and await state regulations while unused medications remain\non the street and abuse continues to grow.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nImmediately.\n\n",
